WEBVTT


00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.500
Downloaded @ subs4free.club


00:00:24.792 --> 00:00:29.073
What I wanted to do first
is to give you an image


00:00:29.097 --> 00:00:32.633
of what drives me
forward in fusion.


00:01:32.693 --> 00:01:36.306
Imagine you're reaching up
and you're grabbing the sun,


00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:39.309
something of the order of a
100 million degrees Celsius,


00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:41.469
and you want to put
the sun in a bottle.


00:01:48.642 --> 00:01:52.822
And we have ways of doing that.
We create a magnetic model.


00:01:52.846 --> 00:01:56.193
Compared to anything that mankind
has ever done in the past,


00:01:56.217 --> 00:01:59.929
be it walking on the moon,
be it decoding DNA,


00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:01.522
this is a great challenge.


00:02:03.391 --> 00:02:06.470
We, as humans, we're lazy.


00:02:06.494 --> 00:02:09.306
In the sense that if we
have a choice of going


00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.808
a very easy path or
very complicated path,


00:02:11.832 --> 00:02:13.343
we would always
go the easy path.


00:02:13.367 --> 00:02:14.911
And that's what we're
doing with energy.


00:02:14.935 --> 00:02:17.181
We're burning fossil fuels,


00:02:17.205 --> 00:02:19.516
coal, natural gas, crude oil,


00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.642
and that is changing
the environment.


00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:35.332
We're similar to, I hate
to say it, to yeast,


00:02:35.356 --> 00:02:37.534
where the yeast basically
multiply, multiply, multiply,


00:02:37.558 --> 00:02:40.537
eat up all of the
flour and the dough


00:02:40.561 --> 00:02:43.840
until basically they die off
from their own excrement,


00:02:43.864 --> 00:02:45.942
uh, because they've just eaten
up all their resources,


00:02:45.966 --> 00:02:48.602
and they've died from their own
pollution that they've created.


00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:07.597
We have to find a clean source


00:03:07.621 --> 00:03:09.799
that's going to be
available for lifetimes,


00:03:09.823 --> 00:03:12.436
and that's what fusion is.


00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:15.004
I really feel that if we don't
crack fusion, we are doomed,


00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.597
to be honest.


00:04:44.852 --> 00:04:46.763
What we're trying to
do here is to make


00:04:46.787 --> 00:04:48.565
a artificial star.


00:04:48.589 --> 00:04:51.067
Stars have some things
we don't have on Earth,


00:04:51.091 --> 00:04:52.793
size and mass.


00:04:53.961 --> 00:04:57.341
The Sun, for example, is a
very nice fusion reactor


00:04:57.365 --> 00:04:59.509
that enjoys considerable
public support,


00:04:59.533 --> 00:05:01.611
and it works on the
basis of gravity


00:05:01.635 --> 00:05:04.948
plus its magnetic field,
uh, to confine the plasma.


00:05:04.972 --> 00:05:07.617
Now, the sun is really big,


00:05:07.641 --> 00:05:10.019
800,000 miles, something
like, across.


00:05:10.043 --> 00:05:12.789
Uh, we clearly don't
want anything that big,


00:05:12.813 --> 00:05:15.616
so we have to rely on the
magnetic fields alone.


00:05:18.819 --> 00:05:20.964
That turns out to be doable,


00:05:20.988 --> 00:05:23.800
but technologically difficult
to do on a large scale.


00:05:23.824 --> 00:05:25.769
And so we've been
learning how to do this


00:05:25.793 --> 00:05:27.595
for about 40 or 50 years.


00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:37.080
What's amazing about work in
that era is we didn't know


00:05:37.104 --> 00:05:40.884
what the energy process
that drove stars was.


00:05:53.487 --> 00:05:56.132
Hans Bethe, the
nuclear physicist,


00:05:56.156 --> 00:05:57.734
uh, was at a seminar,


00:05:57.758 --> 00:06:00.504
and the next day he
was gonna give a talk


00:06:00.528 --> 00:06:03.807
where he talked about a fusion
fuel cycle for the stars.


00:06:03.831 --> 00:06:07.911
But he had a date that night, and
so they went out for a walk.


00:06:07.935 --> 00:06:11.515
And he came out with what has
to be the best line ever,


00:06:11.539 --> 00:06:13.883
which is, "Aren't the
starts beautiful tonight?


00:06:13.907 --> 00:06:16.844
And right now, I'm the only guy
in the world who knows why."


00:06:19.079 --> 00:06:20.156
In the sun's core,


00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:24.428
when these particles are
accelerated to high speeds


00:06:24.452 --> 00:06:25.729
and collide...


00:06:25.753 --> 00:06:27.421
they fuse.


00:06:33.661 --> 00:06:36.573
Fusion fuel is in,
essentially, infinite supply


00:06:36.597 --> 00:06:39.533
and is available to all
countries at negligible cost.


00:06:40.801 --> 00:06:45.815
The fuel, or fusion...
is in common water.


00:06:45.839 --> 00:06:49.553
Fusion will produce no noxious
chemical combustion products,


00:06:49.577 --> 00:06:53.523
and there will be absolutely no
chance of a runaway reaction.


00:06:53.547 --> 00:06:55.559
And fusion will not
involve materials


00:06:55.583 --> 00:06:58.662
that could be stolen or used
for clandestine purposes.


00:06:58.686 --> 00:07:01.731
When I came into this project
almost 20 years ago,


00:07:01.755 --> 00:07:04.534
there was a tremendous
amount of enthusiasm.


00:07:04.558 --> 00:07:06.894
Everyone was confident
of success.


00:07:10.197 --> 00:07:12.075
In the Zeta apparatus,


00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:14.177
we have produced temperatures


00:07:14.201 --> 00:07:16.045
which are about
one-third of those


00:07:16.069 --> 00:07:18.047
at the center of the sun.


00:07:18.071 --> 00:07:19.583
But this enthusiasm


00:07:19.607 --> 00:07:22.018
was a kind of
ignorant enthusiasm.


00:07:22.042 --> 00:07:25.054
As fusion researchers have
painfully discovered,


00:07:25.078 --> 00:07:26.723
it's just not that simple.


00:07:26.747 --> 00:07:28.592
After this initial enthusiasm,


00:07:28.616 --> 00:07:31.728
then came a decade of
caution and skepticism.


00:07:31.752 --> 00:07:35.231
And now, after 20 years,
we're back to enthusiasm.


00:07:35.255 --> 00:07:37.601
In the early 1980s,
we expect to create


00:07:37.625 --> 00:07:38.802
many thermal megawatts


00:07:38.826 --> 00:07:40.861
of fusion energy for
the first time.


00:07:56.243 --> 00:07:58.922
So the whole planet
needs a lot of energy.


00:07:58.946 --> 00:08:02.892
And so far, we've been running
mostly on fossil fuels.


00:08:02.916 --> 00:08:03.927
Been a good run.


00:08:03.951 --> 00:08:06.720
It got us to where we
are, but we have to stop.


00:08:08.021 --> 00:08:10.233
So we are trying different
types of energy now,


00:08:10.257 --> 00:08:11.267
alternative energy.


00:08:11.291 --> 00:08:14.003
But it proved quite
difficult to find something


00:08:14.027 --> 00:08:17.006
that's as convenient
and as cost-effective


00:08:17.030 --> 00:08:20.076
as oil, gas, and coal.


00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:24.581
Now, we know of two way of
making, er, nuclear energy:


00:08:24.605 --> 00:08:26.916
Fission and fusion.


00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.985
Now, in fission, you
take a big nucleus,


00:08:29.009 --> 00:08:32.021
you break it in part, in two,
and it makes lots of energy.


00:08:32.045 --> 00:08:34.924
This is how the nuclear
reactor today works.


00:08:34.948 --> 00:08:36.225
Works pretty good.


00:08:36.249 --> 00:08:37.927
And then there is fusion.


00:08:37.951 --> 00:08:40.730
Now, I like fusion.
Fusion's much better.


00:08:40.754 --> 00:08:43.600
So you take two small
nucleus, you put it together,


00:08:43.624 --> 00:08:44.834
and you make helium.


00:08:44.858 --> 00:08:47.170
And that's very nice.
Makes lots of energy.


00:08:47.194 --> 00:08:50.807
And if the whole planet would
run on fusion, it would run...


00:08:50.831 --> 00:08:52.576
We could extract the
fuel from the ocean,


00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:55.745
it would run for billions
and billions of years.


00:08:55.769 --> 00:08:59.182
Now, if fusion is so great,
why don't we have it.


00:08:59.206 --> 00:09:00.717
Where is it?


00:09:00.741 --> 00:09:02.318
Well, there's always
a bit of a catch.


00:09:02.342 --> 00:09:05.054
Fusion is really,
really hard to do.


00:09:05.078 --> 00:09:08.925
Physics-wise, fusion is
the, the fusion, ha-ha,


00:09:08.949 --> 00:09:09.726
of two nucleus.


00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:12.195
Usually, you use a
hydrogen nucleus


00:09:12.219 --> 00:09:13.797
or isotope of hydrogen.


00:09:13.821 --> 00:09:16.265
And when they touch each
other, they fuse together,


00:09:16.289 --> 00:09:17.500
and they make helium


00:09:17.524 --> 00:09:20.604
and release a lot of energy in
the form of a fast neutron.


00:09:20.628 --> 00:09:23.339
So this is the, the nuclear
reaction in the center.


00:09:23.363 --> 00:09:24.473
Now the problem is
those two nucleus,


00:09:24.497 --> 00:09:26.810
they're electrically charged,
they're both positive,


00:09:26.834 --> 00:09:28.311
so they don't want
to stick together.


00:09:28.335 --> 00:09:30.013
They go like this, whoo, shoo,


00:09:30.037 --> 00:09:31.280
and they never fuse.


00:09:31.304 --> 00:09:33.717
So in order to fuse, you have
to throw them at great speed.


00:09:33.741 --> 00:09:35.952
And speed in a gas
is the temperature.


00:09:35.976 --> 00:09:38.387
So you have to heat the gas
so the thermal agitation


00:09:38.411 --> 00:09:41.691
is fast enough so the nucleus
can touch each other.


00:09:41.715 --> 00:09:44.027
And that temperature is a
150 million degrees C.


00:09:44.051 --> 00:09:46.796
That's pretty hot. And this
is how the stars work.


00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:48.164
The-The stars are very hot


00:09:48.188 --> 00:09:51.067
and inside the star, there
is this fusion reaction.


00:09:51.091 --> 00:09:53.803
So on the Earth here,
we need to heat the gas


00:09:53.827 --> 00:09:55.304
to 150 million degrees C.


00:09:55.328 --> 00:09:59.108
And the other problem is a gas
that hot wants to cool down.


00:09:59.132 --> 00:10:00.744
So it cools down really quickly.


00:10:00.768 --> 00:10:03.379
So you put some energy
in there to heat it up,


00:10:03.403 --> 00:10:04.447
and then the heat escape.


00:10:04.471 --> 00:10:06.850
And when it's hot enough,
it makes some reaction,


00:10:06.874 --> 00:10:08.918
it makes some fusion,
it makes some energy.


00:10:08.942 --> 00:10:10.687
So the name of the
game is to try to get


00:10:10.711 --> 00:10:12.112
more energy out than you put in.


00:11:16.944 --> 00:11:18.788
The world's most
ambitious attempt


00:11:18.812 --> 00:11:21.090
to harvest fusion as
a source of power


00:11:21.114 --> 00:11:22.826
is taking shape in France.


00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:26.262
The project is costing
13 billion pounds


00:11:26.286 --> 00:11:29.198
and it's being backed by a
whole swathe of countries


00:11:29.222 --> 00:11:30.958
around the world.


00:11:52.045 --> 00:11:54.457
ITER's going to be built
from about a million pieces,


00:11:54.481 --> 00:11:55.892
so it's a real nightmare


00:11:55.916 --> 00:11:57.927
to know where pieces are
and don't lose them,


00:11:57.951 --> 00:11:59.495
know which piece goes where,


00:11:59.519 --> 00:12:01.721
wh-what is this piece
that I'm looking at.


00:12:02.823 --> 00:12:04.834
We build the tokamak
from the bottom up,


00:12:04.858 --> 00:12:06.469
so you can only build
it in a certain way.


00:12:06.493 --> 00:12:07.837
So if a certain
piece is missing,


00:12:07.861 --> 00:12:09.238
we'll have to wait for it.


00:12:09.262 --> 00:12:11.540
So it's key... At the
moment, what we're doing


00:12:11.564 --> 00:12:14.243
is a very large planning
effort with all the members


00:12:14.267 --> 00:12:17.046
to make sure that each
piece is delivered to us


00:12:17.070 --> 00:12:18.581
in good time and
at the right time.


00:12:18.605 --> 00:12:21.550
The parties all want to
learn all the technologies,


00:12:21.574 --> 00:12:24.387
which means you not giving
one component to a party,


00:12:24.411 --> 00:12:26.990
you're giving a piece of a
component, and the other party


00:12:27.014 --> 00:12:28.858
wants another piece
of the same component


00:12:28.882 --> 00:12:30.526
so that they all can
learn the technology.


00:12:30.550 --> 00:12:32.996
It is luckily not so that
we have seven pieces


00:12:33.020 --> 00:12:35.564
- of each component that it
will be too difficult...


00:12:35.588 --> 00:12:37.433
but we have sometimes
three or four pieces


00:12:37.457 --> 00:12:38.902
of each component,
and that means...


00:12:38.926 --> 00:12:41.337
We have to talk to our
partners, to the members,


00:12:41.361 --> 00:12:42.471
in all the... as you say,


00:12:42.495 --> 00:12:43.973
in all these countries
around the world,


00:12:43.997 --> 00:12:47.410
in Korea, in Japan, in
Russia, China, US, India.


00:12:47.434 --> 00:12:50.579
You have not one
guy to deal with.


00:12:50.603 --> 00:12:52.415
You have three or four
guys to deal with


00:12:52.439 --> 00:12:53.883
for the same component.


00:12:53.907 --> 00:12:55.919
We are pushing the
edge of technology


00:12:55.943 --> 00:12:58.888
and therefore, sometimes
we run into problems


00:12:58.912 --> 00:13:00.890
and we have to solve
these problems.


00:13:00.914 --> 00:13:04.928
Critics point out that the costs
have trebled in five years.


00:13:04.952 --> 00:13:07.163
They say the whole
project is a gamble


00:13:07.187 --> 00:13:08.397
that won't pay off.


00:13:08.421 --> 00:13:11.835
Will we be able, with
our complex structure,


00:13:11.859 --> 00:13:14.503
and the money we
have, to be in time?


00:13:14.527 --> 00:13:18.207
This is actually the most, uh,
difficult thing at the moment,


00:13:18.231 --> 00:13:19.366
to stay in time.


00:13:20.934 --> 00:13:22.545
What's slowly taking shape here


00:13:22.569 --> 00:13:24.247
in the southern
French countryside


00:13:24.271 --> 00:13:28.117
could provide the answer to
the world's energy crisis,


00:13:28.141 --> 00:13:31.220
or it might prove one of the
most expensive failures


00:13:31.244 --> 00:13:33.013
in scientific history.


00:13:40.187 --> 00:13:42.165
So I'm gonna try to
go through quickly.


00:13:42.189 --> 00:13:44.333
I wanna give you an idea
of the realistic schedule.


00:13:44.357 --> 00:13:48.037
I received an email this morning
of a new first plasma date,


00:13:48.061 --> 00:13:50.006
which is different from
the first plasma date


00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:51.941
that you will see, uh,


00:13:51.965 --> 00:13:54.010
or which was the first plasma
date of about two weeks ago,


00:13:54.034 --> 00:13:55.244
which is different
from what you'll see.


00:13:55.268 --> 00:13:58.347
So there's three iterations
behind the first plasma date.


00:13:58.371 --> 00:14:03.186
When ITER organization
came about in 2006,


00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.621
we said that we would be
producing the first plasma


00:14:05.645 --> 00:14:07.090
in 10 years.


00:14:07.114 --> 00:14:08.591
And if you guys do the
math real quickly,


00:14:08.615 --> 00:14:12.252
you realize that we're not ready
for first plasma next year.


00:14:27.134 --> 00:14:29.269
Not sure where I'm
gonna put this guy.


00:14:30.637 --> 00:14:32.205
It's a toad.


00:14:34.141 --> 00:14:37.653
- Where was that?
- He was inside the compost.


00:14:37.677 --> 00:14:41.190
So he digs underneath...
in the compost pile


00:14:41.214 --> 00:14:42.258
to stay warm.


00:14:42.282 --> 00:14:45.194
My problem is that I turn
the compost all the time,


00:14:45.218 --> 00:14:48.464
so I don't want him
to go in there again.


00:14:48.488 --> 00:14:50.023
I'm gonna put him
underneath here.


00:14:52.993 --> 00:14:54.137
Back when I started in fusion,


00:14:54.161 --> 00:14:58.107
back in the 19... mid-1980s,


00:14:58.131 --> 00:15:01.144
the idea was that fusion
was gonna be successful,


00:15:01.168 --> 00:15:04.413
and we would have
fusion on the grid


00:15:04.437 --> 00:15:06.649
in about 50 years.


00:15:06.673 --> 00:15:10.419
And now, it's, uh,
35 years into my...


00:15:10.443 --> 00:15:12.279
30 years into my career now.


00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:16.125
Up until about 2000,
the year 2000,


00:15:16.149 --> 00:15:18.094
we were just advancing
and advancing


00:15:18.118 --> 00:15:19.953
at really a fast rate.


00:15:20.687 --> 00:15:24.600
And now, the problem
is is that, uh,


00:15:24.624 --> 00:15:26.602
in order to make the next step,


00:15:26.626 --> 00:15:28.504
we just need to go
to a bigger machine


00:15:28.528 --> 00:15:30.406
and unfortunately,
bigger machines


00:15:30.430 --> 00:15:34.077
take longer to build,
are much more complex.


00:15:34.101 --> 00:15:39.215
I know that I will be retiring
before ITER's successful,


00:15:39.239 --> 00:15:42.385
so I'm like the guy
building a cathedral,


00:15:42.409 --> 00:15:45.588
who have... knows that he's
gonna be putting this brick on,


00:15:45.612 --> 00:15:46.755
this brick on, and
this brick on,


00:15:46.779 --> 00:15:48.457
and he's gonna be
spending his whole career


00:15:48.481 --> 00:15:49.558
putting bricks together,


00:15:49.582 --> 00:15:51.427
but he'll never see the
end piece, the cathedral,


00:15:51.451 --> 00:15:53.353
that will take hundreds
of years to build.


00:18:25.538 --> 00:18:29.152
My name is Evgeny Velikov,


00:18:29.176 --> 00:18:35.282
and now my main position
is honorary something.


00:19:09.482 --> 00:19:11.660
Are you optimistic,
Mister President.


00:19:11.684 --> 00:19:13.486
I'm always optimistic.


