If you take... I don't
have a Coke bottle...


00:19:54.961 --> 00:19:56.972
If you take a Coke bottle
and you blow across


00:19:56.996 --> 00:19:58.574
the top of the Coke bottle...


00:19:58.598 --> 00:20:01.310
Do you mind... Can you get
me a Coke bottle downstairs?


00:20:01.334 --> 00:20:03.412
It's down over there.


00:20:03.436 --> 00:20:06.349
I have four minutes, no? How much?
Yes, four minutes.


00:20:06.373 --> 00:20:08.484
Four minutes? Is that okay?
It's downstairs.


00:20:08.508 --> 00:20:10.553
-Do you know where the
coffee machine is? -Yes.


00:20:10.577 --> 00:20:12.721
The gyrotron works pretty
much like a Coke bottle.


00:20:12.745 --> 00:20:15.258
Except instead of blowing across
the top of the Coke bottle


00:20:15.282 --> 00:20:16.459
and changing the resonance,


00:20:16.483 --> 00:20:19.995
uh, it-it... you-you
do a electron beam.


00:20:20.019 --> 00:20:23.299
You have basically a little...


00:20:23.323 --> 00:20:25.033
filament down here,
like in a light bulb.


00:20:25.057 --> 00:20:26.369
It heats up,


00:20:26.393 --> 00:20:29.305
and this beam of
electrons comes up,


00:20:29.329 --> 00:20:30.763
and you get a resonance.


00:20:34.901 --> 00:20:38.013
So just as you take your Coke bottle,
you blow across the top of it,


00:20:38.037 --> 00:20:40.883
and the wind is creating
a compression wave


00:20:40.907 --> 00:20:44.453
and making a resonance, making
the sound go up higher,


00:20:44.477 --> 00:20:47.290
this thing is passing
an electron beam,


00:20:47.314 --> 00:20:48.891
and it creates an
electromagnetic resonance.


00:20:48.915 --> 00:20:52.561
You can almost imagine it like
a bunch of little laser beams,


00:20:52.585 --> 00:20:57.400
that you combine them into
a single coherent beam,


00:20:57.424 --> 00:20:58.825
which is the gyrotron beam.


00:21:00.393 --> 00:21:03.739
And then once this
ignites the plasma,


00:21:03.763 --> 00:21:06.599
the plasma itself heats
it and keeps going.


00:21:13.072 --> 00:21:14.574
That makes sense, kind of.


00:21:16.576 --> 00:21:19.522
Oh, no, doesn't it have to
be a glass one? Oh, man!


00:21:19.546 --> 00:21:21.490
- Well...
- We lose the analogy.


00:21:21.514 --> 00:21:23.025
What do you do with the can?


00:21:23.049 --> 00:21:25.394
- No, not a can.
- A bottle, a bottle.


00:21:25.418 --> 00:21:26.562
Oh, you wanted a bottle?


00:21:26.586 --> 00:21:29.398
Yeah, because you can blow
across the top of the bottle


00:21:29.422 --> 00:21:30.499
to make it resonate.


00:21:30.523 --> 00:21:32.968
And you call yourself
a physicist.


00:21:42.369 --> 00:21:45.948
The greatest challenge is
to align these huge pieces


00:21:45.972 --> 00:21:49.518
to the millimeter tolerance,
to the accuracy that we need.


00:21:49.542 --> 00:21:50.619
It's really the magnets,


00:21:50.643 --> 00:21:53.346
the-the... what we call the
toroidal field magnets.


00:21:54.581 --> 00:21:57.426
These magnets make the main
magnetic field of ITER.


00:21:57.450 --> 00:21:59.695
They're the things that
hold this magnetic bottle,


00:21:59.719 --> 00:22:01.897
hold the plasma
inside the machine,


00:22:01.921 --> 00:22:02.998
and keep it away from the walls.


00:22:03.022 --> 00:22:06.101
And the accuracy that we
can install these magnets


00:22:06.125 --> 00:22:08.937
really is how smooth
the plasma will be


00:22:08.961 --> 00:22:10.373
and therefore how
well it performs.


00:22:10.397 --> 00:22:12.408
So the better we do
and the closer we get


00:22:12.432 --> 00:22:14.977
to the required position
for these magnets


00:22:15.001 --> 00:22:16.845
will really affect, for decades,


00:22:16.869 --> 00:22:18.146
the future performance of ITER.


00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:19.639
So that's a great
challenge for us.


00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:54.009
Well, you need some area,
of course, to absorb...


00:23:54.033 --> 00:23:55.968
- Yeah, yeah.
- The excess material.


00:24:26.065 --> 00:24:27.109
For sure.


00:24:27.133 --> 00:24:28.835
You cannot avoid that.


00:24:35.207 --> 00:24:37.820
I'm curing them. I'm
curing with my hand.


00:24:37.844 --> 00:24:39.879
Ahhh!


00:24:43.783 --> 00:24:46.294
It is not a work for
emotionally weak people,


00:24:46.318 --> 00:24:48.296
because you need to be
very strong and confident


00:24:48.320 --> 00:24:51.233
because otherwise... you
will never do anything.


00:24:51.257 --> 00:24:52.959
You'll just be so scared.


00:25:00.733 --> 00:25:03.579
Although ITER is called


00:25:03.603 --> 00:25:05.748
the largest scientific
enterprise,


00:25:05.772 --> 00:25:09.017
international collaboration
on Earth at the moment,


00:25:09.041 --> 00:25:10.218
nobody knows about us.


00:25:10.242 --> 00:25:12.755
ITER is, um...


00:25:12.779 --> 00:25:14.222
is working with public money,


00:25:14.246 --> 00:25:17.626
so the whole project
is jointly funded


00:25:17.650 --> 00:25:19.628
by all these nations
under the ITER roof,


00:25:19.652 --> 00:25:22.731
and so, in fact
it's the taxpayer


00:25:22.755 --> 00:25:24.266
who pays for what we do, right?


00:25:24.290 --> 00:25:27.069
So this is not a
private organization


00:25:27.093 --> 00:25:28.303
or a private enterprise.


00:25:28.327 --> 00:25:31.574
This is one of our
top benchmarks,


00:25:31.598 --> 00:25:33.776
that we develop this project


00:25:33.800 --> 00:25:37.846
and this fusion reactor for
the benefit of everything.


00:25:37.870 --> 00:25:41.183
And we want everybody
to share and to join in


00:25:41.207 --> 00:25:43.318
and to be proud of this, right?


00:25:43.342 --> 00:25:49.725
So transparency and having
an open-door policy


00:25:49.749 --> 00:25:53.119
is one of our top priorities
in ITER communications.


00:25:55.187 --> 00:25:57.633
A lot of people talk
about a magnetic model,


00:25:57.657 --> 00:25:59.735
and I don't feel there's
a good understanding


00:25:59.759 --> 00:26:01.804
of how the magnetic field ties


00:26:01.828 --> 00:26:04.039
-with this idea of trapping a particle.
-Mm-hmm.


00:26:04.063 --> 00:26:06.041
And then I want to go into
the challenge of ITER.


00:26:06.065 --> 00:26:09.077
The question is, well, how
come we're taking a long time?


00:26:09.101 --> 00:26:10.879
They made a study back in 1976


00:26:10.903 --> 00:26:13.749
that showed that depending
upon how you fund fusion,


00:26:13.773 --> 00:26:16.384
you can either get
a fusion device


00:26:16.408 --> 00:26:19.187
generating electricity
somewhere in the 1990s


00:26:19.211 --> 00:26:22.991
out to 2006 all depending upon
how much money you put in


00:26:23.015 --> 00:26:24.660
as a functional time.


00:26:24.684 --> 00:26:26.629
And then I'm going
to overlay this


00:26:26.653 --> 00:26:29.264
as what the US has
actually been funding.


00:26:29.288 --> 00:26:30.699
And the funding has gone up.


00:26:30.723 --> 00:26:32.868
It peaked around 1978


00:26:32.892 --> 00:26:36.963
and then has dropped well below,
based on today's dollars.


00:26:37.964 --> 00:26:39.174
It's very understandable,
very honest,


00:26:39.198 --> 00:26:42.978
but I won't do this because we
have the US here on Monday,


00:26:43.002 --> 00:26:44.980
uh, physically, and...


00:26:45.004 --> 00:26:46.048
it's a very touchy
thing in the US.


00:26:46.072 --> 00:26:49.251
Because here you're picking
out the US from the...


00:26:49.275 --> 00:26:50.318
We have to be careful we don't,


00:26:50.342 --> 00:26:53.388
in official presentation,
want to criticize, right?


00:26:53.412 --> 00:26:56.825
We can say it's difficult.
Right?


00:26:56.849 --> 00:27:00.328
Because, um, in the 70s,
the funding was wow.


00:27:00.352 --> 00:27:02.731
Everybody wanted
fusion to happen now.


00:27:02.755 --> 00:27:05.000
-Then everything dropped again.
Right? -Poof! Yeah.


00:27:05.024 --> 00:27:07.369
We survived. We managed to
survive, and we're here now


00:27:07.393 --> 00:27:10.038
building the world's
largest fusion device.


00:27:10.062 --> 00:27:12.775
But it's hard, and
it only happens,


00:27:12.799 --> 00:27:16.278
and we say we can deliver, uh,


00:27:16.302 --> 00:27:18.847
around the mid of the century...


00:27:18.871 --> 00:27:21.884
provided that we have
somebody sort of...


00:27:21.908 --> 00:27:24.228
-Funding us in the back.
- funding us in the back. Right?


00:27:48.134 --> 00:27:51.113
And so to me, before
we basically die


00:27:51.137 --> 00:27:53.982
in our excrement like
yeast molecules,


00:27:54.006 --> 00:27:57.085
we need to realize that we
need another energy source.


00:27:57.109 --> 00:27:59.454
We need to be... It may not
be for our generation.


00:27:59.478 --> 00:28:01.924
I mean, the good news
is we're, we're okay.


00:28:01.948 --> 00:28:04.392
But it's the next generation
or the generation after that


00:28:04.416 --> 00:28:05.527
or the generation after that.


00:28:05.551 --> 00:28:08.463
Yeah, I'm actually surprised
how low the funding is.


00:28:08.487 --> 00:28:11.800
Would you be able to have, er...


00:28:11.824 --> 00:28:15.738
better progress, faster progress
if you had better funding?


00:28:15.762 --> 00:28:17.806
I think so. There was a study
made in one country...


00:28:17.830 --> 00:28:19.407
I'm not gonna name
the country...


00:28:19.431 --> 00:28:20.909
Back in the 70s.


00:28:20.933 --> 00:28:24.747
And they said if you keep
the level of the order


00:28:24.771 --> 00:28:26.348
of, let's say, I
think it was about


00:28:26.372 --> 00:28:29.017
800 million euros per year,


00:28:29.041 --> 00:28:33.188
you would be able to achieve
an ITER-like machine,


00:28:33.212 --> 00:28:35.223
uh, within about 20 years.


00:28:35.247 --> 00:28:37.893
Then if you drop it down
to about 500 million,


00:28:37.917 --> 00:28:40.495
it would be extended
out to about 30 years.


00:28:40.519 --> 00:28:42.497
And there is a point
of no return,


00:28:42.521 --> 00:28:46.201
where basically if you
invested 300 million euros


00:28:46.225 --> 00:28:47.169
on a year-to-year basis,


00:28:47.193 --> 00:28:50.105
you would actually...
It would take infinity


00:28:50.129 --> 00:28:51.406
to achieve a reactor.


00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:55.143
Because there you had to keep
the supporting technology base,


00:28:55.167 --> 00:28:57.179
the administrative funds.


00:28:57.203 --> 00:29:01.149
If you go below 300 million,
you'll never get fusion.


00:29:01.173 --> 00:29:02.517
Well...


00:29:02.541 --> 00:29:04.052
that country has never invested


00:29:04.076 --> 00:29:06.088
more than something
like 200 million euros.


00:29:06.112 --> 00:29:09.024
The more money you put into
it, the faster the return.


00:29:09.048 --> 00:29:11.559
And-And we really have
been putting in peanuts.


00:29:11.583 --> 00:29:15.931
I don't think there's a
broad public acceptance


00:29:15.955 --> 00:29:19.567
of hydrogen fusion in the
United States at this stage.


00:29:19.591 --> 00:29:24.339
Uh, people back in the
US still in general


00:29:24.363 --> 00:29:25.874
don't understand the differences


00:29:25.898 --> 00:29:28.410
between uranium fission,


00:29:28.434 --> 00:29:32.347
which is the process that's been
used in nuclear power plants


00:29:32.371 --> 00:29:34.249
for over 40 years now,


00:29:34.273 --> 00:29:36.284
and hydrogen fusion,


00:29:36.308 --> 00:29:39.321
uh, which is believe is the
process of the future.


00:29:39.345 --> 00:29:43.025
You have to keep in mind a
couple of things, you know.


00:29:43.049 --> 00:29:46.561
First, this is one of the grand
challenges of engineering.


00:29:46.585 --> 00:29:51.033
And solving a grand
challenge on a fixed price


00:29:51.057 --> 00:29:54.336
is, is a challenge
in and of itself.


00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:56.271
Now, by the same token,


00:29:56.295 --> 00:30:00.876
you have to be very cost and
schedule conscience... conscious,


00:30:00.900 --> 00:30:04.012
because the sponsors of
this project have to,


00:30:04.036 --> 00:30:06.581
you know, continue to
maintain their sponsorship


00:30:06.605 --> 00:30:08.917
and believe strongly


00:30:08.941 --> 00:30:11.043
that the cost is worth
the investment.


00:30:15.414 --> 00:30:18.560
I actually got interested
in fusion as a child.


00:30:18.584 --> 00:30:22.030
Uh, my mom, when I was eight,


00:30:22.054 --> 00:30:24.599
got me what I think was
my first science book,


00:30:24.623 --> 00:30:26.568
which was about the sun.


00:30:26.592 --> 00:30:29.337
And I still remember
vividly this illustration


00:30:29.361 --> 00:30:34.209
of this hundreds of millions
of miles long coal train


00:30:34.233 --> 00:30:38.881
delivering all the coal
that equaled the energy


00:30:38.905 --> 00:30:42.484
that the sun's fusion
created in a single second.


00:30:42.508 --> 00:30:44.887
By the time I graduated,


00:30:44.911 --> 00:30:47.089
I actually didn't intend
to go into fusion


00:30:47.113 --> 00:30:52.227
because this was the high
point of the enthusiasm


00:30:52.251 --> 00:30:54.029
about the tokamak device,


00:30:54.053 --> 00:30:58.166
which is still the most
funded device in fusion.


00:30:58.190 --> 00:31:00.936
And I thought, "Well, I
guess this is solved,"


00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:03.071
and I got interested instead


00:31:03.095 --> 00:31:05.164
in astrophysics
and other things.


00:31:06.232 --> 00:31:08.643
But, um...


00:31:08.667 --> 00:31:10.946
pretty soon it
became clear, nope,


00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:12.404
it wasn't solved.


00:31:17.009 --> 00:31:20.422
The first error that was made
by the government programs


00:31:20.446 --> 00:31:22.624
back in the 1970s


00:31:22.648 --> 00:31:26.594
was to put all their eggs
in the tokamak basket.


00:31:26.618 --> 00:31:29.331
Now, 40 years later,


00:31:29.355 --> 00:31:34.102
people would have to say,
objectively we do not know


00:31:34.126 --> 00:31:39.107
which route will lead
to practical fusion,


00:31:39.131 --> 00:31:41.944
and we certainly don't
know which route will lead


00:31:41.968 --> 00:31:44.279
to the most economical fusion.


00:31:44.303 --> 00:31:49.384
What you have to do is take
a crash program approach,


00:31:49.408 --> 00:31:51.053
a broad-based approach


00:31:51.077 --> 00:31:54.189
in which, if there are 20
good ideas out there...


00:31:54.213 --> 00:31:56.624
And by good ideas,
I don't mean ideas


00:31:56.648 --> 00:31:58.726
that I think will work.


00:31:58.750 --> 00:32:02.464
I mean ideas that I
can't prove won't work.


00:32:02.488 --> 00:32:06.401
This is an important vital
question for humanity:


00:32:06.425 --> 00:32:10.238
What is an energy source that
can replace fossil fuels,


00:32:10.262 --> 00:32:14.676
that can be safe,
clean, unlimited,


00:32:14.700 --> 00:32:17.045
and more economical,


00:32:17.069 --> 00:32:19.338
cheaper than anything
we have today?


00:32:23.775 --> 00:32:25.520
I used to live on Bowen Island,


00:32:25.544 --> 00:32:26.721
so the commute was great.


00:32:26.745 --> 00:32:28.223
So I could get on my bike


00:32:28.247 --> 00:32:30.349
and it was, like, a 10
minute bike to the garage.


00:32:32.551 --> 00:32:35.530
The part will arrive, and I
would assemble all the parts,


00:32:35.554 --> 00:32:38.100
and I will actually do all
the experiments myself.


00:32:38.124 --> 00:32:40.735
There is only one guy here.
It's a one-guy show.


00:32:40.759 --> 00:32:43.195
So that was actually quite fun.
I enjoyed that.


00:32:44.330 --> 00:32:46.108
And then I would build
this machinery,


00:32:46.132 --> 00:32:49.077
and then I will fire it up, and
then I would get bad result,


00:32:49.101 --> 00:32:50.212
which is most of the
time you get that.


00:32:50.236 --> 00:32:52.614
And then I will tweak it and
adjust with a little click


00:32:52.638 --> 00:32:53.948
and some transfer somewhere.


00:32:53.972 --> 00:32:56.751
And then I started to get some
neutron coming out of this thing.


00:32:56.775 --> 00:32:59.245
So I was very excited
about my neutrons.


00:33:00.146 --> 00:33:02.290
Then, eventually, I went out


00:33:02.314 --> 00:33:04.250
and tried to raise more
money with that thing.


00:33:08.820 --> 00:33:11.199
The company got bigger
and more successful,


00:33:11.223 --> 00:33:13.992
but the fun went
downhill since then.


00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:30.585
Now, after my PhD,
sadly, I did not manage


00:33:30.609 --> 00:33:32.187
to find a job in fusion.


00:33:32.211 --> 00:33:35.290
So I got a job at a local
company doing laser printer,


00:33:35.314 --> 00:33:37.125
because I was kind
of good with lasers.


00:33:37.149 --> 00:33:39.661
Turns out that what
I was trying to do


00:33:39.685 --> 00:33:43.065
was trying to make printing so
cheap that we could cut the forest


00:33:43.089 --> 00:33:45.333
and jam you with
junk mail, you know.


00:33:45.357 --> 00:33:47.135
So that was not
very satisfactory.


00:33:47.159 --> 00:33:49.704
And I was looking at the energy
situation on the planet,


00:33:49.728 --> 00:33:51.005
and it was pretty bad.


00:33:51.029 --> 00:33:54.142
I think we're going 100 miles
an hour towards a brick wall,


00:33:54.166 --> 00:33:56.344
and nobody seems to be paying
much attention, you know.


00:33:56.368 --> 00:33:59.414
And it was actually on my birthday,
the 40-years-old birthday,


00:33:59.438 --> 00:34:02.184
and I decided I had a
terrible midlife crisis,


00:34:02.208 --> 00:34:04.319
and I say, okay,
I-I-I will change.


00:34:04.343 --> 00:34:07.455
I will not do this job
anymore, and I will do fusion.


00:34:07.479 --> 00:34:10.392
In the center of the machine,
there's a big sphere


00:34:10.416 --> 00:34:12.427
and in that sphere,
there is liquid metal.


00:34:12.451 --> 00:34:14.662
Now the liquid metal is
pumped by some pumps


00:34:14.686 --> 00:34:16.098
in those pipes over there,


00:34:16.122 --> 00:34:17.265
and it's made up to swirl.


00:34:17.289 --> 00:34:20.535
So the-the liquid is injected
near the edge like this,


00:34:20.559 --> 00:34:21.703
and it swirls around.


00:34:21.727 --> 00:34:23.805
And because it swirls around,
it appear in the center,


00:34:23.829 --> 00:34:26.474
like a centrifugal force
keep the liquid out


00:34:26.498 --> 00:34:29.211
and near the center... Up
near the center of the thing.


00:34:29.235 --> 00:34:31.746
So we fire those 14 pistons
all at the same time,


00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:33.181
and they're quite
well-synchronized.


00:34:33.205 --> 00:34:37.119
It's gonna go clonk,
clonk, clonk!


00:34:37.143 --> 00:34:39.721
And then the acoustic
waves squash the liquid,


00:34:39.745 --> 00:34:42.290
and it collapse the
vortex over the plasma.


00:34:42.314 --> 00:34:44.592
And the plasma, when you
compress it, will get hotter.


00:34:44.616 --> 00:34:47.729
And hopefully we will hit
150 million degrees C,


00:34:47.753 --> 00:34:48.896
which is the
temperature required


00:34:48.920 --> 00:34:50.732
to make the nucleus to fuse.


00:34:50.756 --> 00:34:52.400
It's piston, and it's rings.


00:34:52.424 --> 00:34:55.537
It's, uh, it's metal and pipes.
It's plumbing.


00:34:55.561 --> 00:34:58.273
Turning that into a power
plant will actually be


00:34:58.297 --> 00:35:00.475
not that complicated.


00:35:00.499 --> 00:35:02.544
I have, I have a saying
here at General Fusion


00:35:02.568 --> 00:35:05.880
I told all my engineer, "If you
can't find it at Home Depot,


00:35:05.904 --> 00:35:07.749
it doesn't go in the machine."


00:35:07.773 --> 00:35:09.617
So the tokamak and the
laser fusion have


00:35:09.641 --> 00:35:11.486
more chance of working
than what we do here,


00:35:11.510 --> 00:35:14.589
because the physics of
compressing the plasma


00:35:14.613 --> 00:35:16.358
with a magnetic field is new.


00:35:16.382 --> 00:35:18.826
However, their chance of
turning out a power plant


00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:20.895
that's cost effective is low,


00:35:20.919 --> 00:35:23.565
because their machine
is so complicated.


00:35:23.589 --> 00:35:26.234
I-I'm quite confident that
we can make this work,


00:35:26.258 --> 00:35:27.669
and I'm a little concerned


00:35:27.693 --> 00:35:30.662
that we might run out of money
before we make it work.


00:36:13.372 --> 00:36:14.782
We have everywhere problems.


00:36:14.806 --> 00:36:17.919
We have had problems
in the TF coils,


00:36:17.943 --> 00:36:19.887
uh, and it was solved.


00:36:19.911 --> 00:36:22.457
Uh, we-we could go
into the envelope


00:36:22.481 --> 00:36:24.692
with the help of Japan,
Europe, and so on.


00:36:24.716 --> 00:36:27.229
So, many things we could bring


00:36:27.253 --> 00:36:29.297
into a reasonable
envelope of schedule,


00:36:29.321 --> 00:36:31.266
but we cannot make miracles.


00:36:31.290 --> 00:36:34.302
Time lost is lost.
I cannot recover.


00:36:34.326 --> 00:36:36.438
It can only be
better from now on.


00:36:36.462 --> 00:36:38.640
The problem that I
don't understand is


00:36:38.664 --> 00:36:40.842
how are you gonna get
there 10 years from now?


00:36:40.866 --> 00:36:42.410
'Cause we heard this morning


00:36:42.434 --> 00:36:44.479
there's a problem with
the vacuum vessel,


00:36:44.503 --> 00:36:47.014
tooling issues, all
kind of things.


00:36:47.038 --> 00:36:50.552
I don't see what the plan is.


00:36:50.576 --> 00:36:53.255
But I thought you have
a much better plan,


00:36:53.279 --> 00:36:55.457
uh, when it comes to
the technologies,


00:36:55.481 --> 00:36:57.449
engineering the solutions.


00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:00.027
Whatever that is, I
mean, convince us


00:37:00.051 --> 00:37:02.530
that I think we can get
there within 10 years.


00:37:02.554 --> 00:37:04.966
This morning when
I asked the DG,


00:37:04.990 --> 00:37:08.303
what was the failure of
this whole problem was,


00:37:08.327 --> 00:37:09.871
and wasting 10 years,


00:37:09.895 --> 00:37:11.406
he said he was a manager.


00:37:11.430 --> 00:37:12.940
Right?


00:37:12.964 --> 00:37:15.301
Is there any change?
Doing anything?


00:38:17.429 --> 00:38:19.574
There's a lot depending
on ITER, right?


00:38:19.598 --> 00:38:24.612
So the whole fusion research
going on all around the world,


00:38:24.636 --> 00:38:27.949
whether it's in China,
in Europe, in the US,


00:38:27.973 --> 00:38:29.351
they all depend on ITER.


00:38:29.375 --> 00:38:31.653
So if ITER is closed down,


00:38:31.677 --> 00:38:34.356
a lot of people will not
only lose their jobs,


00:38:34.380 --> 00:38:38.626
but, um, fusion will be dead


00:38:38.650 --> 00:38:40.695
forever or at least for
a very, very long time.


00:38:40.719 --> 00:38:43.655
Nobody will ever bet on
fusion for a long time.


00:39:02.941 --> 00:39:06.745
So this is our facility where...


00:39:08.013 --> 00:39:13.351
You know, storage facility,
which is actually quite cheap.


00:39:14.085 --> 00:39:15.730
The key thing is,


00:39:15.754 --> 00:39:18.466
more money has to go
into fusion research.


00:39:18.490 --> 00:39:21.168
It has to go for
many other devices,


00:39:21.192 --> 00:39:23.562
obviously including our own.


00:39:27.833 --> 00:39:31.178
We and our neighbors are
shielded against the neutrons


00:39:31.202 --> 00:39:33.481
by three feet of concrete.


00:39:33.505 --> 00:39:36.951
It's also shielded
by this copper mesh


00:39:36.975 --> 00:39:39.987
that radio waves
can't get through.


00:39:40.011 --> 00:39:43.691
Now, the main bang trigger
is basically a big switch.


00:39:43.715 --> 00:39:47.895
When we're at full power,
all 12 will be hooked up.


00:39:47.919 --> 00:39:51.533
These instruments are
actually our thermometer.


00:39:51.557 --> 00:39:53.835
If you wonder how
can you measure


00:39:53.859 --> 00:39:57.505
one or two billion
degrees, this is it.


00:39:57.529 --> 00:40:00.508
Maybe there's some way you
could tape this so that


00:40:00.532 --> 00:40:02.677
- it's out of the line of sight.
- Okay.


00:40:02.701 --> 00:40:06.614
Even though we're a
very tiny group,


00:40:06.638 --> 00:40:09.551
science is a
collaborative effort,


00:40:09.575 --> 00:40:13.087
and we're collaborating through
the scientific literature


00:40:13.111 --> 00:40:14.913
with people all
around the world.


00:40:18.149 --> 00:40:20.161
- Hey!
- Oh, sorry.


00:40:20.185 --> 00:40:22.830
You're on camera.


00:40:22.854 --> 00:40:25.032
This is an important
member of the team.


00:40:25.056 --> 00:40:27.869
This is Tom, who's
the landlord here.


00:40:27.893 --> 00:40:29.427
Hey, guys.


00:40:30.762 --> 00:40:33.140
People ask, "How can you succeed


00:40:33.164 --> 00:40:35.810
with so few resources?"


00:40:35.834 --> 00:40:38.646
You know, are you saying you're
a thousand times smarter?


00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:39.814
No.


00:40:39.838 --> 00:40:42.040
We're saying we've
got an easier route.


00:40:43.709 --> 00:40:44.986
A plasma,


00:40:45.010 --> 00:40:48.490
which is what most of the
universe consists of,


00:40:48.514 --> 00:40:51.225
is electrically
conducting matter,


00:40:51.249 --> 00:40:54.862
matter in which the
electrons are stripped away


00:40:54.886 --> 00:40:57.832
from the atoms and can
freely move about.


00:40:57.856 --> 00:41:00.835
What we today call
the "pinch effect"


00:41:00.859 --> 00:41:06.140
forms instabilities
within the plasma,


00:41:06.164 --> 00:41:07.809
basically pinching the plasma


00:41:07.833 --> 00:41:11.245
into a lot of
filamentary structures.


00:41:11.269 --> 00:41:17.852
The conventional attitude
towards the instabilities


00:41:17.876 --> 00:41:19.053
is to suppress them.


00:41:19.077 --> 00:41:23.481
As we put it, to make the plasma
sit still like a good dog.


00:41:24.783 --> 00:41:26.994
The problem with that is
the plasma doesn't want


00:41:27.018 --> 00:41:28.062
to sit still.


00:41:28.086 --> 00:41:32.133
And trying to confine the
plasma for long enough


00:41:32.157 --> 00:41:34.802
for the fusion reactions
to take place


00:41:34.826 --> 00:41:38.139
becomes sort of like
confining a can of worms


00:41:38.163 --> 00:41:39.698
without the can.


00:41:40.732 --> 00:41:44.846
What we do is to imitate nature.


00:41:44.870 --> 00:41:50.685
In nature, on a scale of...
solar flares,


00:41:50.709 --> 00:41:53.755
quasars, entire galaxies,


00:41:53.779 --> 00:41:57.859
these filaments organize and
structure the universe.


00:41:57.883 --> 00:41:59.551
What's your trigger pressure?


00:42:00.351 --> 00:42:02.053
It's 12.36.


00:42:04.322 --> 00:42:06.625
So when the machine fires,


00:42:07.258 --> 00:42:10.037
we get a ion beam


00:42:10.061 --> 00:42:12.063
that goes down this drift tube.


00:42:13.632 --> 00:42:15.577
Once the machine fires,


00:42:15.601 --> 00:42:17.745
then it's the job of
all our instruments


00:42:17.769 --> 00:42:19.781
to find out what
actually happened.


00:42:19.805 --> 00:42:21.539
Everybody ready?


00:42:22.207 --> 00:42:24.810
Power on. Charging.


00:42:26.678 --> 00:42:29.891
So what we do in
the plasma focus


00:42:29.915 --> 00:42:32.794
is we don't try and fight
these instabilities,


00:42:32.818 --> 00:42:36.063
we try to use them to
compress the plasma


00:42:36.087 --> 00:42:37.088
and to confine them.


00:42:38.356 --> 00:42:39.825
Twenty...


00:42:42.360 --> 00:42:43.729
thirty.


00:42:44.362 --> 00:42:46.097
Set. Fire.


00:42:48.299 --> 00:42:51.212
That means the device
can be much smaller.


00:42:51.236 --> 00:42:53.915
The energy can be much
more concentrated,


00:42:53.939 --> 00:42:56.174
and that makes the
device much cheaper.


00:42:57.709 --> 00:42:59.711
Now, that was a free fire.


00:43:02.080 --> 00:43:03.114
Didn't you hear that?


00:43:06.985 --> 00:43:08.896
I just want to make sure
it's not self-firing.


00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:11.365
There was a slight
ticking noise in there


00:43:11.389 --> 00:43:12.991
I couldn't identify.


00:43:15.193 --> 00:43:16.971
The scope still set?


00:43:16.995 --> 00:43:18.596
They're dry.


00:43:19.865 --> 00:43:21.142
Power on.


00:43:24.035 --> 00:43:25.136
Charging.


00:43:28.974 --> 00:43:30.208
Ten...


00:43:33.144 --> 00:43:34.212
twenty...


00:43:35.413 --> 00:43:36.815
thirty.


00:43:37.448 --> 00:43:39.226
Set. Fire.


00:43:39.250 --> 00:43:40.828
Nope.


00:43:40.852 --> 00:43:45.399
We know of no reason why this
is physically impossible.


00:43:45.423 --> 00:43:48.870
And more, no one has told us


00:43:48.894 --> 00:43:52.406
a reason why they think this
is physically impossible.


00:43:52.430 --> 00:43:54.909
And this project is a
very public project.


00:43:54.933 --> 00:43:57.936
We've published in
peer-reviewed papers...


00:43:59.070 --> 00:44:03.751
that are among the leading
journals in our field.


00:44:03.775 --> 00:44:05.753
We've gotten a lot
of press coverage.


00:44:05.777 --> 00:44:09.290
People have a lot of opportunity
to take potshots at us.


00:44:09.314 --> 00:44:12.426
Some people say this
is a long shot,


00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:14.762
this is way out.


00:44:14.786 --> 00:44:18.900
Well, ultimately, they're just
expressing their feeling.


00:44:18.924 --> 00:44:23.695
I do say that compared
with all the other...


00:44:25.163 --> 00:44:26.774
private fusion efforts...


00:44:26.798 --> 00:44:30.068
our results at present
are the best.


00:44:32.103 --> 00:44:35.941
So, here-here... Tom just
became our latest shareholder.


00:44:36.775 --> 00:44:39.077
Yeah.


00:44:42.981 --> 00:44:44.783
Okay...


00:44:56.361 --> 00:45:00.074
It's my pleasure to
welcome all of you.


00:45:00.098 --> 00:45:01.809
I introduce myself.


00:45:01.833 --> 00:45:05.947
I'm the director-general
of the ITER organization.


00:45:05.971 --> 00:45:08.816
It is really my
honor and pleasure


00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:13.888
to address you here
today in this role.


00:45:13.912 --> 00:45:19.150
This little project as you know
is a very promising project,


00:45:20.886 --> 00:45:25.023
which now gathers
seven large parties.


00:45:25.857 --> 00:45:27.534
All of them as you know,


00:45:27.558 --> 00:45:30.171
they're representing
over 85 percent


00:45:30.195 --> 00:45:33.775
of the gross national
product in the world,


00:45:33.799 --> 00:45:36.010
and all of them are
very keen to understand


00:45:36.034 --> 00:45:40.514
how they will get their energy
supply in the long term.


00:45:40.538 --> 00:45:44.185
All of you know that there
is renewable energies,


00:45:44.209 --> 00:45:48.522
and we are very keen to see
these renewable energies


00:45:48.546 --> 00:45:50.982
to move on, to progress.


00:45:52.083 --> 00:45:55.529
But for the time
being, it is clear


00:45:55.553 --> 00:45:57.531
that it will not be able


00:45:57.555 --> 00:46:00.401
to fulfill the
expectations of the world.


00:46:00.425 --> 00:46:02.193
Oh, sorry.


00:46:03.895 --> 00:46:06.097
I will... I will say stop.


00:46:07.332 --> 00:46:08.766
Hello?


00:46:24.549 --> 00:46:26.027
Sorry.


00:46:26.051 --> 00:46:28.295
We are always
interconnected, so...


00:46:28.319 --> 00:46:33.034
As you know, renewable
energy is good for...


00:46:33.058 --> 00:46:34.969
I think we all
have great respect


00:46:34.993 --> 00:46:36.570
for what he's trying to do here,


00:46:36.594 --> 00:46:37.638
what he has to do.


00:46:37.662 --> 00:46:40.298
We are all standing behind
him, to help him to...


00:46:41.532 --> 00:46:43.510
to get the big puzzle together.


00:46:43.534 --> 00:46:45.112
It's different, right?


00:46:45.136 --> 00:46:48.182
I mean, the first two
director-generals were Japanese


00:46:48.206 --> 00:46:50.885
and certainly, um,


00:46:50.909 --> 00:46:53.354
different cultures have
different approaches


00:46:53.378 --> 00:46:55.322
to... to work with,


00:46:55.346 --> 00:46:56.924
uh, different priorities,


00:46:56.948 --> 00:46:59.894
and Mr. Bigot is a European.


00:46:59.918 --> 00:47:02.163
So for us Europeans,
it's certainly easier,


00:47:02.187 --> 00:47:04.065
right, to understand
what he wants.


00:47:04.089 --> 00:47:07.434
But if it is possible
to demonstrate


00:47:07.458 --> 00:47:10.037
and it will work for, okay,


00:47:10.061 --> 00:47:12.039
thousand and thousand of euros,


00:47:12.063 --> 00:47:13.975
I'm ready to wait for 20 years.


00:47:13.999 --> 00:47:15.433
Is not my problem.


00:47:19.037 --> 00:47:21.048
No, no, no, you know,
the sun is not obsolete


00:47:21.072 --> 00:47:22.616
even after five
billion of years.


00:48:00.645 --> 00:48:02.489
Well, I think there is something


00:48:02.513 --> 00:48:03.991
inherently difficult
about fusion.


00:48:04.015 --> 00:48:06.393
That it is, uh, an
attempt to harness


00:48:06.417 --> 00:48:09.496
some of the most difficult-
to-harness forces in nature.


00:48:09.520 --> 00:48:11.232
You have to get something


00:48:11.256 --> 00:48:14.135
at tens or hundreds of
millions of degrees Kelvin


00:48:14.159 --> 00:48:16.938
into a tight package, and
nature resists that.


00:48:16.962 --> 00:48:18.605
So tha-that is inherently hard.


00:48:18.629 --> 00:48:21.399
But on top of that, um...


00:48:22.567 --> 00:48:24.678
there seem to be
political pressures


00:48:24.702 --> 00:48:28.015
trying to blow apart
any large project.


00:48:28.039 --> 00:48:31.052
That once you have to
gather many, many people


00:48:31.076 --> 00:48:35.957
from different countries
to combine resources,


00:48:35.981 --> 00:48:38.425
especially over
decades and decades,


00:48:38.449 --> 00:48:42.629
the different political
wills, the different goals,


00:48:42.653 --> 00:48:45.032
uh, all clash with each other


00:48:45.056 --> 00:48:47.601
and eventually you wind
up with infighting,


00:48:47.625 --> 00:48:49.270
with cost overruns,


00:48:49.294 --> 00:48:52.306
and things begin to fall
apart after several years.


00:48:52.330 --> 00:48:54.976
And this is what we saw
with ITER round one,


00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:56.710
and the same thing seems
to be happening again,


00:48:56.734 --> 00:48:58.403
uh, with round two.


00:49:09.414 --> 00:49:11.592
I am often asked the question,


00:49:11.616 --> 00:49:13.627
how do you maintain
a sense of urgency


00:49:13.651 --> 00:49:16.430
on a project that takes
20 years or more?


00:49:16.454 --> 00:49:21.202
Yeah? And so the answer
is that globally...


00:49:21.226 --> 00:49:23.204
'Cause for this crowd,
the Apollo Project


00:49:23.228 --> 00:49:25.206
is what they always
think about as the...


00:49:25.230 --> 00:49:29.010
That if we think about
how short for most of us


00:49:29.034 --> 00:49:31.145
20 years ago really seems and
all that has happened...


00:49:31.169 --> 00:49:34.181
The Secretary of Energy
was asked specifically


00:49:34.205 --> 00:49:37.218
to make a recommendation by
the second of May this year


00:49:37.242 --> 00:49:39.386
on that topic, a
progress report on ITER,


00:49:39.410 --> 00:49:41.788
and should the US stay
in or should they not.


00:49:41.812 --> 00:49:45.392
Yeah, I think that
it's quite clear


00:49:45.416 --> 00:49:48.262
that, uh, things are moving
in a positive direction,


00:49:48.286 --> 00:49:51.732
but we were digging out of
a fairly significant hole,


00:49:51.756 --> 00:49:54.468
and so progress was
really necessary.


00:49:54.492 --> 00:49:57.304
It's up to the American
Congress to make the decision,


00:49:57.328 --> 00:50:00.598
you know, if the
project will deliver.


00:50:11.509 --> 00:50:14.588
Sub-Committee on Energy
will come to order.


00:50:14.612 --> 00:50:18.059
And we wanna welcome you to
today's hearing entitled,


00:50:18.083 --> 00:50:21.128
"An Overview of Fusion
Energy Science."


00:50:21.152 --> 00:50:23.364
Is it ee-ter or eye-ter?


00:50:23.388 --> 00:50:25.299
- Ee-ter.
- Ee-ter. Hmm. Okay.


00:50:25.323 --> 00:50:27.801
Tokamak. I keep wanting
to say "Tomahawk,"


00:50:27.825 --> 00:50:29.403
and I know that's...


00:50:29.427 --> 00:50:30.771
that's not right.


00:50:30.795 --> 00:50:34.441
With the complexity of a multi-national
collaboration like ITER,


00:50:34.465 --> 00:50:38.579
this project has faced
more challenges than most.


00:50:38.603 --> 00:50:41.082
Fortunately, today, we have
the opportunity to hear


00:50:41.106 --> 00:50:43.350
from the director-general of
the ITER project directly,


00:50:43.374 --> 00:50:46.353
Dr. Bernard... Is it Bee-go?


00:50:46.377 --> 00:50:49.256
Uh, I do believe if we have
the proper management,


00:50:49.280 --> 00:50:51.825
we will be able to
deliver on time.


00:50:51.849 --> 00:50:53.694
We have spent how much money


00:50:53.718 --> 00:50:55.496
over the last ten years,
the United States?


00:50:55.520 --> 00:50:57.698
When the US signed
up for the project,


00:50:57.722 --> 00:50:59.366
you know, the
representation was made


00:50:59.390 --> 00:51:01.202
that this project
was ready to go


00:51:01.226 --> 00:51:02.769
to an extent that,
in retrospect,


00:51:02.793 --> 00:51:03.937
probably wasn't the case.


00:51:03.961 --> 00:51:08.575
When you think about what we
spent on imported oil alone...


00:51:08.599 --> 00:51:12.313
I just want to understand
the dimensions of the cliff


00:51:12.337 --> 00:51:14.815
that we're playing near when we
talk about the US pulling out.


00:51:14.839 --> 00:51:19.186
If you're gonna have to lay
odds on, on all the engineering


00:51:19.210 --> 00:51:21.488
and all these things
coming together,


00:51:21.512 --> 00:51:24.225
uh... what are your odds?


00:51:24.249 --> 00:51:27.728
I do believe this project
could be so beneficial


00:51:27.752 --> 00:51:30.764
to the world, that
it is really worth


00:51:30.788 --> 00:51:32.733
to try and to demonstrate.


00:51:32.757 --> 00:51:34.601
- And again...
- Let me mention this.


00:51:34.625 --> 00:51:38.172
There are a lot of wonderful
things we can do in this world.


00:51:38.196 --> 00:51:40.541
- I know.
- Wonderful things.


00:51:40.565 --> 00:51:41.875
- And...
- Including ITER.


00:51:41.899 --> 00:51:44.611
Yeah, um, okay. And ITER
may be one of them,


00:51:44.635 --> 00:51:48.615
but what we do is we judge
each one based on the cost


00:51:48.639 --> 00:51:51.618
and the chances of success.


00:51:51.642 --> 00:51:55.256
Because, in theory, it's such
a beautiful and simple idea.


00:51:55.280 --> 00:51:57.391
Yet, throughout,
people have found


00:51:57.415 --> 00:52:00.627
as soon as they get
around the next corner,


00:52:00.651 --> 00:52:02.763
there is yet another hurdle.


00:52:02.787 --> 00:52:05.699
Um, and nature seems
to have this way


00:52:05.723 --> 00:52:07.668
of throwing up, um,


00:52:07.692 --> 00:52:11.238
blockade after blockade
after blockade that makes


00:52:11.262 --> 00:52:14.632
scientists' optimism,
uh, look naive.


00:52:16.567 --> 00:52:19.446
My favorite fusion conman,


00:52:19.470 --> 00:52:22.440
uh, was Richter from Argentina.


00:52:25.776 --> 00:52:28.489
He was a German or
Austrian expat...


00:52:28.513 --> 00:52:30.924
We actually know very
little about him...


00:52:30.948 --> 00:52:33.960
Who moved to Argentina


00:52:33.984 --> 00:52:36.330
and managed to
convince Juan Peron


00:52:36.354 --> 00:52:37.831
that he had figured out a method


00:52:37.855 --> 00:52:40.801
of, uh, harnessing the sun


00:52:40.825 --> 00:52:42.427
and called it thermotron.


00:52:43.728 --> 00:52:46.631
And so they built this
secret lab on an island.


00:52:47.565 --> 00:52:52.313
And this crazy expat
was running around


00:52:52.337 --> 00:52:53.947
pouring gunpowder
in experiments,


00:52:53.971 --> 00:52:55.849
blowing doors off his lab,


00:52:55.873 --> 00:52:58.319
writing fusion on ticker
tapes everywhere,


00:52:58.343 --> 00:53:01.555
convincing everyone for a
matter of months and years


00:53:01.579 --> 00:53:04.782
that he has solved the
world's energy problems.


00:53:17.928 --> 00:53:20.807
Until he was finally found out
as a fraud and wound up in jail.


00:53:25.336 --> 00:53:28.014
One of the things that's
interesting about his story


00:53:28.038 --> 00:53:31.485
is that the physicists


00:53:31.509 --> 00:53:34.788
who first thought
about magnetic fusion


00:53:34.812 --> 00:53:35.989
were inspired by his story.


00:53:36.013 --> 00:53:40.827
They saw a front page
New York Times claim


00:53:40.851 --> 00:53:43.897
about fusion energy
in Argentina,


00:53:43.921 --> 00:53:45.599
and the scientists
here were thinking,


00:53:45.623 --> 00:53:47.358
how could that be done?


00:53:55.633 --> 00:53:58.345
My wife and I were planning
to leave for Aspen,


00:53:58.369 --> 00:53:59.646
and my father, he said,


00:53:59.670 --> 00:54:02.516
"Well, I understand the
Argentines have, uh,


00:54:02.540 --> 00:54:05.386
have gotten ahead of you
in the fusion program."


00:54:05.410 --> 00:54:08.522
He'd seen the New
York Times article


00:54:08.546 --> 00:54:12.326
that a fellow named Richter
had released fusion


00:54:12.350 --> 00:54:14.027
in a controlled
manner in Argentina,


00:54:14.051 --> 00:54:16.430
and Peron made quite
a thing of it.


00:54:16.454 --> 00:54:17.964
I read the New York
Times article.


00:54:17.988 --> 00:54:21.392
Then we got on a
train down to Aspen.


00:54:32.002 --> 00:54:35.682
It was during the intervals out
there, when we weren't skiing,


00:54:35.706 --> 00:54:37.484
I was thinking about this.


00:54:37.508 --> 00:54:41.388
How would one do it if
one were trying to?


00:54:41.412 --> 00:54:44.491
I worked out some of
the general ideas


00:54:44.515 --> 00:54:48.786
that I later developed
into the stellarator.


00:54:50.388 --> 00:54:53.033
So Spitzer came up
with this cool device


00:54:53.057 --> 00:54:54.568
called the stellarator.


00:54:54.592 --> 00:54:57.070
Stellarators are a
complicated machine,


00:54:57.094 --> 00:54:59.740
and they have worse
confinement in general.


00:54:59.764 --> 00:55:02.743
But there's no such thing as
disruption in a stellarator.


00:55:02.767 --> 00:55:05.746
And so stellarators
for the past decade


00:55:05.770 --> 00:55:07.548
have just been more
and more complicated.


00:55:07.572 --> 00:55:10.016
They look like squid fighting
each other at this point.


00:55:10.040 --> 00:55:13.820
You can specify what magnetic
field configuration you want.


00:55:13.844 --> 00:55:16.823
So then you give the computer
the job of iterating


00:55:16.847 --> 00:55:18.525
through a bunch of
possible different coils


00:55:18.549 --> 00:55:21.362
until it gets to the right
magnetic field configuration.


00:55:21.386 --> 00:55:23.830
And you end up with
something weird, like this.


00:55:23.854 --> 00:55:25.198
It's cool, right?


00:55:25.222 --> 00:55:28.426
All right, any other questions
before we move on to the tokamak?


00:55:29.494 --> 00:55:30.895
Okay, let's bust a move.


00:55:53.484 --> 00:55:56.029
So W7-X is a really
cool experiment.


00:55:56.053 --> 00:55:58.031
It's a stellarator which has


00:55:58.055 --> 00:55:59.633
a lot of advantages
over a tokamak.


00:55:59.657 --> 00:56:01.668
The biggest advantage is
that it can just run.


00:56:01.692 --> 00:56:05.739
It just... You know,
for hours hopefully.


00:56:05.763 --> 00:56:08.609
And once you can do that, you
can build a power plant.


00:56:08.633 --> 00:56:11.745
Um, and so tokamaks right
now have a problem


00:56:11.769 --> 00:56:14.080
where they just, they
can't run continuously,


00:56:14.104 --> 00:56:16.049
but this experiment is
designed to show that


00:56:16.073 --> 00:56:17.183
it can be done continuously


00:56:17.207 --> 00:56:20.887
at parameters that are good
enough for fusion, so...


00:56:20.911 --> 00:56:23.189
I came here, and I came to
know about stellarators


00:56:23.213 --> 00:56:25.158
and somewhere along
the line, I switched


00:56:25.182 --> 00:56:28.795
from believing in tokamaks
to more in stellarators,


00:56:28.819 --> 00:56:30.096
and I thought, yeah,


00:56:30.120 --> 00:56:31.989
maybe this is the future
of energy and fusion.


00:56:35.092 --> 00:56:38.171
This project would not
have been possible


00:56:38.195 --> 00:56:39.906
without the reunion of Germany.


00:56:39.930 --> 00:56:44.044
It is co-financed by
Europe to a big extent.


00:56:44.068 --> 00:56:47.213
But still the project
team is in our hands.


00:56:47.237 --> 00:56:50.517
We had to take care that
we have young people


00:56:50.541 --> 00:56:53.086
being able to do physics
now with the new machine


00:56:53.110 --> 00:56:56.590
and really to make this a team.


00:56:56.614 --> 00:57:01.595
Wendelstein is a highly
optimized stellerator device,


00:57:01.619 --> 00:57:02.963
three-dimensional design.


00:57:02.987 --> 00:57:05.065
It goes back about 20 years.


00:57:05.089 --> 00:57:09.002
And, um, to build this, to get
the support to build this,


00:57:09.026 --> 00:57:12.673
they had to build it in the
then-reuniting Germany.


00:57:12.697 --> 00:57:14.708
It's a crucial
part of the story.


00:57:14.732 --> 00:57:16.977
So they started a
completely new institute


00:57:17.001 --> 00:57:18.545
from nothing up here.


00:57:18.569 --> 00:57:22.849
And they had to go into building something
that had never been built before.


00:57:22.873 --> 00:57:27.187
Now, unsurprisingly, it
ended up being more complex


00:57:27.211 --> 00:57:29.980
and expensive and difficult
than they planned.


00:59:56.994 --> 00:59:59.372
Big emotion. Big
emotion, you know.


00:59:59.396 --> 01:00:01.908
I do believe it's
really a landmark


01:00:01.932 --> 01:00:06.747
in the history of fusion to
see with these stellarators


01:00:06.771 --> 01:00:08.381
it works right away.


01:00:08.405 --> 01:00:09.716
Very good.


01:00:09.740 --> 01:00:11.918
She's a really great scientist,


01:00:11.942 --> 01:00:15.355
and she know how to organize
the work of this people


01:00:15.379 --> 01:00:17.423
to give good priorities,


01:00:17.447 --> 01:00:19.793
and I really believe she deserve


01:00:19.817 --> 01:00:21.962
full recognition for
her achievement.


01:00:35.700 --> 01:00:37.277
Well, this fusion business


01:00:37.301 --> 01:00:39.780
has been going a little slower
than we were all hoping


01:00:39.804 --> 01:00:41.114
when we started this company.


01:00:41.138 --> 01:00:42.282
We started with great gusto,


01:00:42.306 --> 01:00:44.317
"Yeah, we're gonna
whisk that up."


01:00:44.341 --> 01:00:46.419
And then we ran
into difficulties,


01:00:46.443 --> 01:00:49.055
so it's a little longer
than what we wanted.


01:00:49.079 --> 01:00:51.291
So it's a little bit
more challenging.


01:00:51.315 --> 01:00:55.095
But we-we're still optimistic
that we're doing good progress.


01:00:55.119 --> 01:00:56.763
It's going a little
slower than I'd like,


01:00:56.787 --> 01:00:58.298
but it's-it's advancing forward.


01:00:58.322 --> 01:00:59.499
So we're not kind of stopped.


01:00:59.523 --> 01:01:03.369
So mood is okay, but now we're,
we're kind of gearing up


01:01:03.393 --> 01:01:07.373
for a bit more of a long run
than a, than a quick success.


01:01:07.397 --> 01:01:09.209
We're certainly not
the startup anymore,


01:01:09.233 --> 01:01:13.413
like we used to be a real
scruffy little startup,


01:01:13.437 --> 01:01:15.882
but now we're getting
more people,


01:01:15.906 --> 01:01:18.484
it's a bigger shop, and
been here for a few years.


01:01:18.508 --> 01:01:20.144
We have shown some result.


01:01:21.145 --> 01:01:23.389
This is 1,500 microseconds.


01:01:23.413 --> 01:01:24.958
The compression time is only


01:01:24.982 --> 01:01:27.894
180 microseconds, a
little time here.


01:01:27.918 --> 01:01:29.329
So the magnetic field is
going up and up and up.


01:01:29.353 --> 01:01:33.066
This is good. This is what we want
when you compress magnetic field.


01:01:33.090 --> 01:01:35.468
But then again you see
all those oscillations,


01:01:35.492 --> 01:01:38.171
both on the outside sensor
and the inside sensor


01:01:38.195 --> 01:01:40.073
and then the plasma crash,


01:01:40.097 --> 01:01:41.141
like it-it dies here.


01:01:41.165 --> 01:01:43.944
What we want it to do is
to go up, up, up, up, up,


01:01:43.968 --> 01:01:45.979
probably to about
this height here.


01:01:52.777 --> 01:01:56.823
So, we're clamping
it now, um, Claude,


01:01:56.847 --> 01:01:59.259
so stop flowing. Stop flowing!


01:01:59.283 --> 01:02:01.886
- Okay!
- Okay.


01:02:03.287 --> 01:02:04.865
This is always a
stressful event,


01:02:04.889 --> 01:02:06.332
because if we let air
into the chamber,


01:02:06.356 --> 01:02:09.069
it's filled with moisture, and it
will ruin the quality of our vacuum.


01:02:09.093 --> 01:02:10.895
It will take weeks and
weeks to recover.


01:02:13.497 --> 01:02:14.999
Ooh, gosh.


01:02:23.507 --> 01:02:26.286
All right, go on, cut
the flow in... now.


01:02:26.310 --> 01:02:28.254
Cut it now.


01:02:28.278 --> 01:02:30.123
Very good. Thanks.


01:02:30.147 --> 01:02:30.991
We did it.


01:02:31.015 --> 01:02:32.993
Five years ago when
we were staring up


01:02:33.017 --> 01:02:34.460
and building our first machines,


01:02:34.484 --> 01:02:36.029
we were having things
blow up every day.


01:02:36.053 --> 01:02:38.398
It was... People were scared.


01:02:38.422 --> 01:02:40.224
The siren really made
people frightened.


01:02:44.261 --> 01:02:46.206
There's no point building
the wrong thing though.


01:02:46.230 --> 01:02:49.109
I got it. Yeah, absolutely.
I know. I know.


01:02:49.133 --> 01:02:51.878
But the flip side of that
is that that can go on


01:02:51.902 --> 01:02:53.914
- for a while, right?
- Mm-hmm.


01:02:53.938 --> 01:02:56.817
I know you got a good handle
on it, but just I gotta ask.


01:02:56.841 --> 01:03:00.020
But-But I'm not against
actually restarting our...


01:03:00.044 --> 01:03:02.022
I... well...


01:03:02.046 --> 01:03:05.525
Because in my opinion, we should
try all the available targets.


01:03:05.549 --> 01:03:07.493
- Got it. However...
- Because you don't know


01:03:07.517 --> 01:03:08.962
what the hell would work or not.


01:03:08.986 --> 01:03:10.396
It it decided that we
have a pot of work,


01:03:10.420 --> 01:03:13.366
and we're trying to fill... we're
trying to put people in those pots.


01:03:13.390 --> 01:03:16.369
No, no, no. We don't have enough
people to fill in those pots,


01:03:16.393 --> 01:03:19.172
so we wanted to get people
to put into those pots


01:03:19.196 --> 01:03:20.874
but what we have done...


01:03:20.898 --> 01:03:23.443
- is make an extra pot.
- Is made a new pot.


01:03:23.467 --> 01:03:26.446
- Be careful of that.
- Yes, I understand the problem.


01:03:28.405 --> 01:03:29.449
Good.


01:03:29.473 --> 01:03:31.051
I-I like... I'm a,


01:03:31.075 --> 01:03:33.586
I'm a practical physicist,


01:03:33.610 --> 01:03:34.720
an experimental physicist.


01:03:34.744 --> 01:03:37.858
It is true that at work I
don't do the work anymore.


01:03:37.882 --> 01:03:40.093
Now, all my guy do the
work, and we just...


01:03:40.117 --> 01:03:42.295
I push paper and answer emails.


01:03:42.319 --> 01:03:46.066
Sometime I go into the lab and turn
some screws just because I like it.


01:03:46.090 --> 01:03:48.368
But yeah, this is one of
the hobby that I have.


01:03:55.966 --> 01:03:57.610
I put my hat. That's
my mother hat.


01:03:57.634 --> 01:03:58.634
She knitted this one.


01:04:00.204 --> 01:04:03.974
Oh, long time ago. Must
be at least 30 years.


01:04:04.608 --> 01:04:06.176
She passed away now.


01:04:07.644 --> 01:04:09.146
She won't knit me a new one.


01:04:59.964 --> 01:05:01.507
My name is Mark Uhran.


01:05:01.531 --> 01:05:04.444
I was previously the director


01:05:04.468 --> 01:05:06.412
of the International
Space Station division


01:05:06.436 --> 01:05:09.582
at NASA headquarters,
and I spent 28 years


01:05:09.606 --> 01:05:11.584
working on the space
station program,


01:05:11.608 --> 01:05:14.687
and I now work for
the US ITER project.


01:05:14.711 --> 01:05:19.159
What struck me the strongest
from the very first year


01:05:19.183 --> 01:05:20.626
as I began to meet the people


01:05:20.650 --> 01:05:22.662
that were engaged
around the world


01:05:22.686 --> 01:05:24.164
was how much this was like


01:05:24.188 --> 01:05:26.699
the International Space
Station program.


01:05:26.723 --> 01:05:30.370
Uh, ITER, when I joined,
was right around the end


01:05:30.394 --> 01:05:33.173
of their final design phase.


01:05:33.197 --> 01:05:35.308
And today they're on the cusp


01:05:35.332 --> 01:05:38.444
between final design
and construction.


01:05:38.468 --> 01:05:40.580
And the space station program


01:05:40.604 --> 01:05:43.616
toward the end of the
final design phase


01:05:43.640 --> 01:05:46.119
was really quite chaotic.


01:05:46.143 --> 01:05:50.123
Uh, they had not yet
reached a point


01:05:50.147 --> 01:05:53.193
where the
decision-making process


01:05:53.217 --> 01:05:55.095
was being done


01:05:55.119 --> 01:05:58.498
in accordance with practices
of systems engineering.


01:05:58.522 --> 01:06:00.700
It took two to three years


01:06:00.724 --> 01:06:02.335
to accomplish that turnaround,


01:06:02.359 --> 01:06:07.140
because this was a project
that spanned five partners


01:06:07.164 --> 01:06:09.775
and involved on the order of
five to ten thousand people


01:06:09.799 --> 01:06:11.144
around the world.


01:06:11.168 --> 01:06:13.413
Oh, it's like trying
to change the course


01:06:13.437 --> 01:06:15.148
on a supertanker.


01:06:15.172 --> 01:06:17.217
So when I joined
ITER four years ago,


01:06:17.241 --> 01:06:20.053
the process was
similarly chaotic


01:06:20.077 --> 01:06:22.222
and certainly in need of


01:06:22.246 --> 01:06:25.658
a regimented and disciplined
systems engineering approach.


01:06:25.682 --> 01:06:28.228
And over the past four years,


01:06:28.252 --> 01:06:30.487
I've seen progress
in that direction.


01:06:32.056 --> 01:06:35.601
This-This is a real test


01:06:35.625 --> 01:06:36.636
of human civilization.


01:06:36.660 --> 01:06:40.364
And it's a test that we
can't afford to fail.


01:06:42.399 --> 01:06:46.170
We have to prove that we
have the intelligence...


01:06:47.071 --> 01:06:48.572
to prevent our own extinction.


01:09:23.693 --> 01:09:25.938
To be able to work on
fusion, I think is for me


01:09:25.962 --> 01:09:27.640
is a dream come true,


01:09:27.664 --> 01:09:28.941
but it's not just us.


01:09:28.965 --> 01:09:30.443
It's actually you guys as well,


01:09:30.467 --> 01:09:32.612
because your support,
or your taxes,


01:09:32.636 --> 01:09:34.647
are putting force to this,


01:09:34.671 --> 01:09:37.283
to the dawn of the fusion age.


01:09:37.307 --> 01:09:40.286
And so, to a large extent,
I thank you guys,


01:09:40.310 --> 01:09:42.288
one, for your
curiosity in coming,


01:09:42.312 --> 01:09:44.424
but also for your
support for fusion.


01:09:44.448 --> 01:09:46.683
So, thank you.


01:09:47.617 --> 01:09:50.396
- I'm Chuck Flanigan.
- Hi, Chuck.


01:09:50.420 --> 01:09:51.331
Hi.


01:09:51.355 --> 01:09:53.966
Back during the
engineering design phase,


01:09:53.990 --> 01:09:56.369
I was the deputy manager
for the US team.


01:09:56.393 --> 01:09:58.504
- Oh, fantastic!
- That was a long time ago.


01:09:58.528 --> 01:10:01.407
I've been retired
since 95, so...


01:10:01.431 --> 01:10:03.576
The Munich television
people come out.


01:10:03.600 --> 01:10:06.279
They wanted to know, "How can
you be working on something


01:10:06.303 --> 01:10:08.681
that's going to last 50
years into the future?"


01:10:08.705 --> 01:10:11.584
And Ken said, "Have you ever
been into the cathedral?"


01:10:11.608 --> 01:10:12.918
Yeah, exactly.


01:10:12.942 --> 01:10:14.687
Those people never saw
the final product.


01:10:14.711 --> 01:10:17.423
-No, no, they passed away,
but they also... -Good job.


01:10:17.447 --> 01:10:20.426
- People like Spitzer, same way.
- Yeah. Right. Exactly.


01:10:20.450 --> 01:10:21.861
- Thank you. Also thank you...
- Yeah, thanks.


01:10:21.885 --> 01:10:25.355
For laying the foundation in the
road to get to where we are today.


01:10:26.956 --> 01:10:32.705
I came on February 14, 19...
Uh, 2008.


01:10:32.729 --> 01:10:36.342
I left CRPP Lausanne,
Switzerland one day


01:10:36.366 --> 01:10:38.286
and showed up here the
next day to start working.


01:10:40.036 --> 01:10:41.881
This is cool.


01:10:41.905 --> 01:10:43.373
I get to go on-site.


01:10:47.477 --> 01:10:48.988
I've never gone
beyond this point.


01:10:49.012 --> 01:10:50.490
This is like Frodo, you know.


01:10:50.514 --> 01:10:52.358
I've never been... This
is like, you know,


01:10:52.382 --> 01:10:53.759
a first step on the site.


01:10:53.783 --> 01:10:55.385
This is cool, isn't it?


01:11:00.657 --> 01:11:01.825
Go this way?


01:11:05.529 --> 01:11:07.373
I look at this and I see...


01:11:07.397 --> 01:11:10.510
what is gonna happen
in five, ten years.


01:11:10.534 --> 01:11:13.078
So even though I haven't
been down there,


01:11:13.102 --> 01:11:15.339
I know what it is in my brain.


01:11:22.078 --> 01:11:23.956
I can see the vessel.


01:11:23.980 --> 01:11:25.825
I can see inside the vessel.


01:11:25.849 --> 01:11:28.828
I can see everything, but I
can't see the plasma side.


01:11:28.852 --> 01:11:31.788
That one, that one I'm
looking forward to seeing.


01:11:33.557 --> 01:11:34.334
Okay?


01:11:34.358 --> 01:11:36.436
Let's go. I wanna
get down there.


01:11:36.460 --> 01:11:37.694
You guys are holding me back.


01:11:40.697 --> 01:11:43.700
This is pretty cool, when you
think also that we're, uh...


01:11:45.134 --> 01:11:47.437
gonna be about 30
meters underground.


01:11:48.104 --> 01:11:49.949
It's weird to be here.


01:11:49.973 --> 01:11:53.619
And to think that in about...


01:11:53.643 --> 01:11:54.787
ten years' time, there's
going to be about


01:11:54.811 --> 01:11:58.424
100 million degrees Celsius above...
sitting above us.


01:11:58.448 --> 01:12:01.427
And another four and a half
meters up is the level 1,


01:12:01.451 --> 01:12:04.029
the ground floor... launchers.


01:12:04.053 --> 01:12:05.898
The next level up, level 2,


01:12:05.922 --> 01:12:07.567
the diagnostics, the
heating systems,


01:12:07.591 --> 01:12:09.935
the ion cyclotron,
electron cyclotron.


01:12:09.959 --> 01:12:11.737
It's all there.


01:12:11.761 --> 01:12:14.330
Just give it some time.
It will come.


01:15:21.117 --> 01:15:25.064
I think that ITER
will probably work,


01:15:25.088 --> 01:15:28.801
and it will demonstrate
that fusion is doable.


01:15:28.825 --> 01:15:31.637
I think that as a project
it's very difficult


01:15:31.661 --> 01:15:32.805
because it's international,


01:15:32.829 --> 01:15:36.175
so there's all sort of project
management issue there.


01:15:36.199 --> 01:15:37.677
So they're gonna
blow their budget


01:15:37.701 --> 01:15:39.869
and their schedule big time.


01:15:47.010 --> 01:15:50.189
It's just become such a
Tower of Babel in there,


01:15:50.213 --> 01:15:54.226
it will burn money at twice the
rate that you need to do it.


01:15:54.250 --> 01:15:56.228
But we'll, we'll
plow through it.


01:15:56.252 --> 01:15:58.964
It will get built,
and it will work.


01:15:58.988 --> 01:16:01.266
And this will, in my
opinion, give a big shot


01:16:01.290 --> 01:16:03.268
in the arm of fusion
because here is a machine


01:16:03.292 --> 01:16:04.804
that showed it can be done.


01:16:04.828 --> 01:16:07.039
However, as a power plant,


01:16:07.063 --> 01:16:08.741
I don't think it's
very practical.


01:16:08.765 --> 01:16:10.643
It's a machine
that's very complex,


01:16:10.667 --> 01:16:12.077
very expensive,


01:16:12.101 --> 01:16:14.880
a little unreliable, there's
those disruption in there


01:16:14.904 --> 01:16:16.882
that happen that can
damage the machine.


01:16:16.906 --> 01:16:19.351
It's-It's difficult to conceive


01:16:19.375 --> 01:16:21.654
that such a machine
can become a reliable


01:16:21.678 --> 01:16:23.155
day-to-day power plant.


01:16:23.179 --> 01:16:26.626
But, you know, it's-it's like
the Wright brothers, you know.


01:16:26.650 --> 01:16:28.694
Like that plane didn't have
to take a 100 passengers


01:16:28.718 --> 01:16:29.795
across the Atlantic,


01:16:29.819 --> 01:16:33.165
but it evolved when somebody
showed that you can fly.


01:16:33.189 --> 01:16:35.901
After that, lots of
investment and money goes in.


